Quoting  Ohle Claussen  <claussen@dpi.physik.uni-goettingen.de>:
>On 2005-10-21, David Damerell wrote:
>>Quoting  Ohle Claussen  <claussen@dpi.physik.uni-goettingen.de>:
>>>I find the idea of any kind of "clock" like this in the game really
>>>repelling. Being able to take as much time as one wants is a major plus
>>>for nethack.
>>In the sense that it permits one to largely obviate the need to
>>demonstrate any skill in the endgame.
>Well, pudding farming is a way to exploit the endlessness of the
>resource "time" in NH in such a way, yes. 
>But many people enjoy the possibility to take their time exploring.
>Newbies have been mentioned, extinctionists usually spend a lot of turns
>before waking Rodney, and those turns are certainly not spent to make
>the endgame easier.

I don't think it needs to be a problem for new players. My first ascension
took 85,000-odd turns, and isn't even my slowest. I don't think a clock to
restrict indefinite character optimisation would be a problem simply
because a genuinely new player isn't aware of all the ways one *can*
optimise indefinitely. Certainly the first time I ascended I pressed
straight on to Gehennom etc. because that was all I knew there was to do.

I certainly don't mind if we break extinctionist games preserving the
challenge in regular games.

>Imagine a not-too-spoiled player falling into the wrong room in the
>rogue quest. Or just a stroke of bad luck with BoH accidents and the
>like that force you to reassamble your kit a few times. 

This is why I propose a soft not a hard clock; if that does happen the
game will become tougher, rather than the Amulet disappearing and the game
being lost. When it comes down to it BoH accidents aren't bad luck,
they're mistakes; and you have to expect mistakes to make the game harder
- many mistakes kill you.

>Pudding farming certainly is a problem that has to be solved, yes. The
>possibility to guess that a certain game involved pudding farming is
>just a workaround, agreed. 
>But so is a clock that enforces a time limit on the game.

A time limit is no more arbitary than a food limit, a hitpoint limit, or a
limit on pre-Quest alignment changes; there just doesn't happen to be one
at the moment. The dungeon gets more dangerous as you descend and as you
gain experience levels; why not as time passes?
